package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part mystery about a school radio archive and a forgotten community project. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch40 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_frequency_of_small_things",
            title = "The Frequency of Small Things",
            tagline = "A school radio team follows a strange broadcast and discovers the stories that connect their town.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "A Voice after the Weather", listOf(
                    "The school radio club had one rule: never broadcast anything that had not been checked. That rule came from their teacher, Mrs Okafor, who had once watched a harmless rumour spread across the school because somebody repeated an overheard sentence without context. So when Kemi and Daniel heard a faint voice interrupt the weather recording one Thursday afternoon, they did not rush to announce that their equipment had picked up a secret message. They saved the recording, wrote down the time, and asked the club technician to check the cables. The voice lasted only four seconds. It said, 'If you can hear this, the map is still waiting.' Then came a soft click and the ordinary weather report continued.",
                    "Daniel replayed the clip through headphones. Behind the voice, he noticed a repeating sound like a bicycle bell. Kemi heard something else: a short musical pattern, three notes rising and one falling. Neither could tell whether the sounds came from the recording itself or from a nearby device. Their club had recently borrowed an old audio mixer from the community centre, and its settings were unfamiliar. Mrs Okafor asked them to keep the file private until they understood what it contained. She also reminded them not to investigate buildings or equipment without permission. The mystery could be solved through records, conversations, and careful listening.",
                    "The next morning, the technician found that the mixer had briefly switched to an unused input channel. It was connected to a small archive receiver that the community centre had donated with the equipment. The receiver was supposed to be disconnected, but its cable had been left in place. That explained how the voice entered the broadcast; it did not explain who had recorded it or what the map meant. Kemi labelled the file 'Unidentified archive audio' rather than giving it a dramatic name. Daniel searched the equipment folder and found a faded sticker: COMMUNITY FREQUENCY PROJECT, 2008. They had their first reliable lead."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Box of Old Recordings", listOf(
                    "Mrs Okafor contacted the community centre and arranged a supervised visit. The centre coordinator, Mr Bassey, welcomed the students into a small media room where old microphones and labelled storage boxes were kept. He explained that the Community Frequency Project had been a local radio experiment. Residents recorded short announcements, interviews, music introductions, and practical information for people who could not attend public meetings. The recordings were stored for educational use, but the project had ended when its volunteers moved away. Mr Bassey gave the students access to a catalogue, not the private recordings themselves, and asked them to note any item they wanted an adult coordinator to review.",
                    "The catalogue listed hundreds of clips by date and topic. Kemi searched for the three-note musical pattern, while Daniel looked for entries mentioning a map. They found a programme called 'Four Corners of Our Town', recorded in the same month as the mystery message. Its description mentioned a walking map made by young volunteers. Another entry, 'Sounds We Nearly Forget', listed a bicycle bell among its background recordings. The clues seemed connected, but the catalogue could not prove that the clips belonged together. Kemi wrote down the reference numbers and asked Mr Bassey whether the project had kept a paper copy of its walking map.",
                    "He searched a cabinet and found a folder containing meeting summaries, a blank map template, and a note about a planned listening trail. The trail was meant to guide visitors between four public places where residents could hear short stories about local history. Each stop had a sound symbol: a bell, a market call, a school whistle, and a ferry horn. The project had run out of volunteers before the trail was completed. Mr Bassey thought the map might still exist in the centre's display archive, but he could not promise it. Daniel looked at Kemi. The strange message might not be a warning at all. It could be an invitation to finish an old project."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "Three Notes and a Bell", listOf(
                    "Back at school, Kemi compared the mystery recording with the catalogue descriptions. The three rising-and-falling notes matched a short audio logo used by the old project. The bicycle bell appeared in a separate sound-effects file, recorded near the market. The students asked the technician to check whether the receiver had combined two archived clips. He found that the receiver had been set to play a saved sequence, not a live signal. That meant the voice had been recorded deliberately, although the date remained uncertain. The team created a simple listening log with the time stamp, file name, and observations. They separated confirmed details from guesses so that a future listener could repeat their work.",
                    "Mrs Okafor reviewed the log and approved a second visit to the centre. This time, Mr Bassey played the public educational clips for them. One recording featured a market trader explaining how the old market bell signalled the start of the weekly produce exchange. Another included a school caretaker describing the whistle used during a community sports day. The third was a ferry worker recalling the horn that helped passengers recognise the evening crossing. Each recording ended with the same three-note logo. The students recognised the pattern, but the mysterious voice was not among the clips selected for public listening.",
                    "Mr Bassey checked the catalogue again and found a reference marked 'Map introduction — rough edit'. Its notes said that a young presenter had recorded several possible endings, one of which mentioned a map waiting to be completed. The rough edit had not been cleared for public use because the project team wanted to confirm the locations first. Mr Bassey said they would need permission from the archive committee before hearing it. Kemi was disappointed, but she understood. The voice belonged to someone who had made a recording, and the students had no right to treat every stored file as theirs to publish. They submitted a request explaining their school project and waited for approval."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "The Map with Four Empty Circles", listOf(
                    "The archive committee approved a supervised listening session and provided a scan of the old map template. Four circles marked the proposed listening stops, but the labels were blank. Around the edges, someone had drawn tiny symbols: a bell, a basket, a whistle, and a curved line that might represent water. Kemi noticed that the symbols matched the catalogue entries. Daniel suggested that the map had been left unfinished because the team could not agree on the route. Mr Bassey said that was possible, but the meeting notes mentioned a different problem: the organisers wanted to make sure the route was accessible and that each recording could be heard without blocking a public walkway.",
                    "The rough audio edit began with the three-note logo, followed by the voice they had heard in the school studio. A second voice said, 'The map is not a list of places. It is a list of people who made room for one another.' Then the recording stopped. There was no name or date. The committee had kept the clip because it was part of the project's creative process, but it had never been approved as a final announcement. Kemi asked whether they could quote the sentence in their report. The committee said they could describe it as an unattributed line from an unfinished recording, provided they made that status clear.",
                    "Daniel studied the four symbols again. He realised they might refer to sounds rather than landmarks. A bell could be recorded at the market, a basket might represent the produce exchange, a whistle could belong to the school sports field, and the curved line might mark the ferry crossing. The map was not necessarily asking people to visit a building. It might be asking them to listen for the ordinary sounds that connected different parts of town. Kemi liked that interpretation, but they still needed evidence. They decided to interview current staff at the four public locations, with permission, and ask whether any part of the old project was remembered."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "The Market's Morning Bell", listOf(
                    "Their first interview took place at the market office, where the manager explained that the bell was now used only on special event days. The weekly produce exchange had changed over time, and the old signal was no longer needed. He remembered a group of students who had recorded the sound many years earlier. They had asked permission, kept out of the traders' way, and shared a copy of the finished recording with the market office. The manager found a printed photograph from the project. In it, a young presenter held a microphone beside the bell, while another student carried a folder with a hand-drawn map tucked inside.",
                    "The photograph did not show the present-day route, but it helped Kemi identify the same three-note logo on a small badge worn by the presenter. The market manager did not know the student's name. He suggested checking the public programme from the year of the recording. The office had kept a copy because the project had been included in a community festival. Daniel found the programme in a drawer and read the list of participating groups. The radio project had been coordinated by a youth club, a school media class, and the community centre. No individual presenter was named.",
                    "Before leaving, Kemi asked whether the market would allow a new recording of the bell for the school's project. The manager agreed, provided the recording happened outside trading hours and the final clip was reviewed before sharing. The students recorded a short sample with the technician present. They also asked the manager to explain what the bell meant, rather than presenting the sound as a mysterious signal. Their first stop was complete. On the bus back to school, Daniel noticed that the old photograph showed a second object beside the folder: a small card with four circles. The map might have been carried from one location to another."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "The Whistle and the Ferry Horn", listOf(
                    "At the school sports field, the caretaker remembered the old project immediately. He said the student presenters had recorded the whistle during a practice session, then interviewed the coach about how teams organised themselves. The whistle was not a signal for danger; it was simply a way to gather players before an activity. The caretaker showed them a public photograph of the recording day. In the background, a noticeboard displayed a schedule with four coloured marks. Kemi copied the colours into her notes and asked whether the school still had the original schedule. The caretaker checked with the head teacher and arranged for the media club to review it later.",
                    "The ferry office was their next stop, accompanied by Mrs Okafor. The supervisor explained that the horn had once been used to announce departures, but modern safety procedures relied on several forms of communication. The old recording was still useful as a historical sound, although it should not be mistaken for a current travel signal. Daniel asked whether the project team had recorded an interview with a ferry worker. The supervisor found a public transcript in the office archive. It described how the crossing connected families, shops, and schools on opposite sides of the water. The transcript also mentioned a listening station that had been planned near the waiting area but never installed.",
                    "The two interviews changed the students' understanding of the map. Each sound had a practical role, but the recordings were really about the people who used those places and helped others move through the day. Kemi drew four circles in her notebook and wrote a question beside each: What does this sound help people do? Daniel added a fifth question: Who should approve the story before it is shared? They were no longer trying to recreate the old project exactly. They were learning how to complete its idea responsibly, using current information and asking permission at every step."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "A Name in the Programme", listOf(
                    "The head teacher found the old sports schedule in a box of media-club materials. It listed the youth club's project coordinator as Adaeze Nwosu. The name also appeared in the festival programme beside the words 'student editor'. Kemi wondered whether Adaeze had recorded the mysterious message. Mrs Okafor explained that a name in a programme was a lead, not proof. They contacted the youth club through its current public email address and asked whether anyone could pass a message to former members. They did not search for private accounts or publish the name. A week later, the club replied that Adaeze had volunteered to speak with them.",
                    "Adaeze joined a video call arranged by the youth club coordinator. She was pleased that the students had found the recordings, but she warned them that the old project had been more complicated than the surviving documents suggested. The team had wanted to make a map that included not only landmarks but also small acts of help: a trader who kept a safe space for lost children while their guardians were contacted, a caretaker who opened a room for community meetings, and ferry workers who helped passengers understand the timetable. The map was meant to celebrate cooperation, not identify private individuals or turn ordinary kindness into a competition.",
                    "Kemi played the four-second message with permission. Adaeze recognised her own voice, though she could not remember which draft it belonged to. She explained that the phrase about the map waiting had been written for a final programme that was never completed. The recording had been saved on the archive receiver by mistake when the equipment was packed away. She had not intended it as a secret clue. Daniel laughed softly at how dramatic the sentence sounded without its context. Adaeze offered to share the original project notes, but only the pages that were approved for public use. The mystery finally had an answer, and the students now had a more meaningful question: what would a modern version of the listening trail look like?"
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "A Trail That Listens", listOf(
                    "Adaeze sent the approved notes through the youth club coordinator. They described a listening trail with four stops and a simple principle: every recording should help visitors notice something useful about the community. The old team had planned short clips, printed captions, and a map that could be followed without a smartphone. They had also considered a quiet alternative for visitors who did not want to use headphones in public. Kemi and Daniel brought the notes to Mrs Okafor, who suggested they design a small school exhibition rather than install anything around town. A school exhibition could be tested, supervised, and reviewed before anyone considered a larger project.",
                    "The students divided the work into four stations. The market station would explain how the bell once organised the produce exchange. The school station would describe the role of the sports whistle. The ferry station would explain how the horn had been used and why current travel information must come from official notices. The final station would feature the community centre's three-note logo and a short account of the radio project's history. Each station would include a printed transcript, a large-text version, and a note identifying the source of the information. Visitors could listen or read. No one would be asked to share a personal story to take part.",
                    "They sent each draft to the relevant office for review. The market manager corrected the description of the bell. The school caretaker confirmed the sports-field details. The ferry supervisor asked them to add a clear note that the recording was historical and not a departure announcement. Mr Bassey approved the archive description and Adaeze checked the explanation of the unfinished map. The process took longer than Kemi expected, but it made the exhibition more accurate. Daniel said the old project had waited years for a finished trail; a few more days of careful checking seemed reasonable."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "The Broadcast Goes Quiet", listOf(
                    "On the morning of the exhibition, the radio club tested every station. The market bell played clearly, but the ferry horn was too loud through the small speaker. The technician lowered its volume and checked the transcript against the recording. Then the school network stopped responding, which meant the digital map could not load. Kemi felt the familiar pressure to fix everything quickly. Mrs Okafor reminded the team that the printed map and transcripts were the main exhibition materials. The digital version was an extra, not the only way to participate. They placed the paper copies on the tables and continued the final checks.",
                    "A group of younger students arrived early and began following the map. One asked why the four sounds were chosen. Daniel explained that each had once helped people coordinate an ordinary activity. Another student noticed that the map did not include the library, even though the library had helped the radio club prepare. Kemi admitted that the old project had only four planned stops, but the exhibition could collect suggestions for a future edition. She placed a blank card beside the map with a heading: What other community sound helps people work together? Students could leave an idea without writing their names.",
                    "The exhibition opened with a short broadcast from the school studio. Kemi introduced the project, explained the source of each recording, and made clear that the four-second message came from an unfinished archive draft. She did not call it a hidden transmission or claim that the old team had predicted the future. Daniel played the three-note logo, then invited visitors to explore the stations at their own pace. The network returned halfway through the event, but nobody had been waiting for it. The paper materials had worked exactly as planned. The team had learned that a good project should not collapse when one optional feature fails."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "The Frequency of Small Things", listOf(
                    "After the exhibition, the radio club counted the anonymous suggestion cards and grouped them by theme. Visitors had mentioned the library's closing bell, the sound of a community football match beginning, the call that announced a public clean-up, and the gentle chime used by a local learning centre. The students did not publish the suggestions as facts about the whole town. They described them as ideas from exhibition visitors and sent the summary to the community centre. Mr Bassey said the archive committee would consider a second exhibition if the relevant organisations approved the recordings. Adaeze offered to help review the historical notes, but she did not ask to be named as the person who solved the mystery.",
                    "The school decided to keep the exhibition materials in its media room and make a copy available to the community centre. The original archive recording remained labelled as an unfinished draft, with a note explaining its context. The receiver was disconnected from the broadcast mixer so another accidental playback would not confuse listeners. Kemi and Daniel added a short guide about checking sources, protecting privacy, and asking permission before recording in public places. Mrs Okafor said the guide might be the most valuable part of the project because it would help the next radio team work with care.",
                    "A month later, the club received an invitation to present its listening exhibition at a youth media gathering. Kemi packed the printed map, Daniel checked the audio levels, and the technician tested the speakers one final time. The old message no longer sounded like a secret. It sounded like a reminder that a community is made from small acts that are easy to overlook until someone takes time to listen. As the three-note logo played, Kemi looked at the four circles on the map. They were no longer empty. Each one held a sound, a source, and a story that had been checked before it was shared."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
